    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Three Card Brag',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Teen Patti (तीन पत्ती), meaning "three cards" in Hindi, is a popular Indian vying game that combines elements of poker and bluffing. Also known as **Flash** or **Three Card Brag**, it is one of the most widely played card games in the Indian subcontinent. The game is traditionally played during festivals, especially Diwali, and has gained international recognition through online gaming platforms.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Country/Region of Origin: India\nTime Period: Several centuries old, exact origins unknown\nEtymology: "Teen" means three and "Patti" means cards in Hindi\nAlternative Names: Flash, Flush, Three Card Brag (British equivalent)',
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
      {
        type: 'rules',
        tabLabel: 'Rules',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Rules',
            subtitle: 'How to play',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'heading',
                level: 3,
                text: 'Objective',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Win the pot by having the best three-card hand or by being the last player remaining after all others have folded.',
              },
              {
                type: 'heading',
                level: 3,
                text: 'Gameplay',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '1. Ante/Boot Phase\nAll players place the agreed minimum stake (boot) into the pot\nThis ensures there is something to play for in each hand',
              },
              {
                type: 'heading',
                level: 4,
                text: 'Key Rules',
              },
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Blind Player: Has not looked at their cards',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Must bet at least the current stake (minimum) or up to twice the current stake (maximum)',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Can bet less than a seen player',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Seen Player: Has looked at their cards',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Must bet at least twice the current stake (minimum) or up to four times the current stake (maximum)',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Must bet double what a blind player bets',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
      {
        type: 'strategy',
        tabLabel: 'Strategy',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Strategy',
            subtitle: 'Tips and guidance',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "For Beginners\n1. Start as a Seen Player: Look at your cards to understand hand values\n2. Play Strong Hands Aggressively: Bet and raise with trails, pure sequences\n3. Fold Weak Hands: Don't chase with high card only\n4. Learn Hand Rankings: Memorize the order of hands\n5. Manage Your Bankroll: Don't bet more than you can afford to lose",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Blind Play Strategy\nAdvantages: Lower betting requirements, psychological pressure on seen players\nWhen to Play Blind: Early in the game, with small stakes, or to confuse opponents\nWhen to Look: When stakes get high or when considering a sideshow',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Bluffing Techniques\nSemi-Bluff: Bet aggressively with drawing hands (potential sequences)\nPure Bluff: Represent strong hands with weak cards\nTiming: Bluff when opponents show weakness\nTable Image: Establish a tight image to make bluffs more credible',
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
